WEBVTT

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.600
Do you hear?  Hooves beating

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.750
A carriage goes through the gentle hills

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.980
Someone comes to pick me up

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:45.950
And takes me

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.900
To the avenue with white flowers

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.050
To the home of wind

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.400
He takes me

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
I'm sure

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.150
Thank you, Mister.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:53.280
Don't mention it.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.550
Good evening, did my paper arrive?

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.650
Yes. The family herald it's already inside.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:08.600
Thank you.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:20.860
What?

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.270
This is for me!

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:27.910
My!

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.600
What happened?

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.900
Oh! An invitation for tea!

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.250
Thank you, mister!

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.580
Anne goes home dancing up the lane,
like a wind-blown sprite,

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:07.520
through the mellow sunshine and
lazy shadows of the August evening.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:12.760
Oh, Marilla!

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:16.360
Oh, Marilla! I have big news!

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.920
What are your eyes popping out
of your head about?

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.460
Have you discovered another kindred spirit?

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:24.310
No. But can you guess what happened?

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:25.470
I don't know.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.130
I am invited to tea at the
manse tomorrow afternoon!

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:31.780
Mrs. Allan left the letter
for me at the post office.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.380
Just look at it.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:38.280
'Miss Anne Shirley, Green Gables.'

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.630
That is the first time I was ever called 'Miss.'
Such a thrill as it gave me!

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:47.770
I shall cherish it forever among
my choicest treasures.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.830
Mrs. Allan told me

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:54.780
she meant to have all the members of
her Sunday-school class to tea in turn.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.180
That just means tomorrow
is your turn, Anne.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.740
B-but, Marilla...

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:09.030
But Mrs. Allan is the first
person to call me Miss.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.510
So her invitation is...

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:10.970
You needn't get in such a fever over it.
So her invitation is...

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.620
You needn't get in such a fever over it.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:18.360
Do learn to take things calmly, child.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.240
Take things calmly, Marilla?

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.160
How can I remain calm after this?

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:27.340
Mrs. Allan invited me out for tea!

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.210
Where are you going?
We have to prepare dinner.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:36.880
I want to show Matthew this letter.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.220
Really.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.760
Matthew, the wind is strong outside.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:05:00.300
Right, if this goes on
it looks like it'll rain tomorrow.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:01.840
Rain?

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:02.910
Is that true, Matthew?

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:08.870
Well now, the wind is round northeast,
so it will probably rain tomorrow.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.600
It can't be!
Right in the day I was invited!

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.840
Matthew, has your forecast ever been wrong?

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:20.980
Well now, most of the
time they prove to be accurate.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:23.860
What should I do?

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:27.980
What's wrong, Anne?

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:29.170
Anne?

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:32.160
What's wrong with her, Matthew?

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:36.830
Well, I... I don't know.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.990
I only said it would rain tomorrow...

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:47.850
My, my. She really needs to
learn to calm down.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:55.360
Anne went to bed that night
speechless with misery.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:05:59.830
Is it raining?

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.000
Everything's over.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:19.420
If it rains tomorrow then
morning should not come.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:29.560
The rustle of the poplar leaves
about the house worried her.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.700
It sounded so like pattering raindrops,

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:38.330
and the full, faraway roar of the gulf
seemed like a prophecy of storm.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:50.310
But the morning, in spite of
Matthew's predictions,

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:54.630
was fine and Anne's spirits
soared to their highest.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.360
What a splendid morning!

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.530
Matthew!

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.110
Matthew! Did you see today's weather?

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.890
Well now, the weather has
turned for the better.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.010
You're right, I saw it too, Matthew!

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:38.570
The morning sun is shooting
its rays behind the clouds.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:43.280
Like... Like if it was smiling.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.840
Because it played
a serious joke on me yesterday.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.320
But matthew, the sun is shy
so it won't apologize.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:56.780
It will just peep at me and blush.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:01.860
Well now, Anne. That makes
me happy even after this bad prediction.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:05.120
Matthew, does that mean your
predictions aren't always right?

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:10.940
But I'm really glad!

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:14.510
You're right. My predictions
aren't always right.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:32.860
Oh, Marilla, there is something in me today
that makes me just love everybody I see.

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:36.510
You don't know how good I feel, do you?

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.610
Wouldn't it be nice if it could last?

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:43.800
I believe I could be a model child if I
were just invited out to tea every day.

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:47.030
I don't mind you being good, but
please don't break the dishes.

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:52.120
But oh, Marilla, it's a solemn occasion too.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.910
I feel so anxious.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.600
What if I shouldn't behave properly?

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.810
You know I never had tea at
a manse before,

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:04.200
and I'm not sure that I know
all the rules of etiquette.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.550
I've been studying the rules given in

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:09.680
the Etiquette Department of the
Family Herald every day, but...

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:15.710
I'm so afraid I'll do something silly or
forget to do something I should do.

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:17.320
The hot water is here.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:19.990
Yes, thank you.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:28.800
Marilla,

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.220
would it be good manners

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:33.030
to take a second helping of anything
if you wanted to VERY much?

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:40.510
The trouble with you, Anne, is that you're
thinking too much about yourself.

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:50.870
You should just think of Mrs. Allan and what
would be nicest and most agreeable to her.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:54.270
You are right.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:00.680
Marilla. I'll try not to think
about myself at all.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:04.170
I'll try to only think about
what would make Mrs. Allan happy.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.680
They look like jewels.

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:19.530
Oh, those are so big.
Like red strawberries.

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.390
But now it's easier to pick them.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:06.400
Now I understand how Eddie Wells
worked hard for his feelings.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:09.070
And I fell in the water when...

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:13.380
More importantly than that,
hurry up and go change your clothes.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.310
Look! the water is dripping!

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:19.540
All right, Marilla.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:22.710
But I'm sure Mrs. Allan will love them,

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:25.460
because they're all big. Look.

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:26.460
Anne!

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:28.240
Yes...

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:39.390
That afternoon...

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:45.010
Anne went to the manse
and took the wild strawberries.

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:13.010
It feels like I have a drum under my chest.

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.300
Anne Shirley's heartbeat.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:18.610
Listen. No matter how good Mrs. Allan is...

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:22.520
No matter how tasty the tea is...

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:25.420
Don't do anything impulsive.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.980
You're Anne Shirley, aren't you?
Welcome, please come in!

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:42.300
What's wrong, Anne?

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:54.210
G-good afternoon, Mrs. Allan.
Thank you for your invitation.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:58.980
You've come at the right time.
I was coming to get you.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:02.910
I was trying to calm down.

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:03.910
And then...

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:07.150
So that's why you stood there.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:10.720
Well, please relax and come in,
Miss Anne Shirley.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:12.810
Miss Anne Shirley...

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:32.710
There was another little
girl at the manse to tea,

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.550
from the White Sands Sunday school.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:39.780
Her name was Laurette Bradley, and
she was around the same age as Anne.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:45.710
I picked this wild strawberries for you.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:47.660
My, wild strawberries?

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:51.930
So many and so big.

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:54.600
Thank you, Anne Shirley.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:56.570
It must have been
a lot of work picking them.

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:58.080
Not really.

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:02.770
But I fell into the river
when I was trying to pluck them.

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:04.570
You fell in the river?

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:05.390
Yes.

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:09.100
I was too careless.
Miss Marilla is always saying that to me.

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:14.260
My mother often scolded me like
that when I was your age too.

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:16.770
Is that true, Mrs. Allan?

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:18.030
Yes, it is.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:19.970
And, were you hurt?

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:22.010
No.
I am well, as you can see.

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:26.500
Then please, both of you sit down.
I'll bring the tea.

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:33.430
With Mrs. Allan's warm smile,
Anne lost her anxiety.

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:36.370
Her heart didn't leap out

00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:42.450
and her first tea invitation was exactly
like 'Miss Anne Shirley' had imagined.

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:47.110
Anne's wild strawberries were
placed on the table,

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:51.990
and she felt very happy
and proud of herself.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:56.580
Hello. Welcome, Anne.

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:00.660
Thank you for your invitation, Mr. minister.

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:03.360
Your voice is very pretty.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:06.050
Anne's voice is pretty good.

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:09.100
Should we let her join the
Sunday-school choir?

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:14.330
Now that I think about it, your friend
Diana Barry sings in the choir, doesn't she?

00:17:14.330 --> 00:17:16.990
Yes. Diana's voice is angelic.

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:21.300
Oh. Mrs. Allan, I've longed so to sing
in the Sunday-school choir,

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:24.230
but I feared it was an
honor I could never aspire to.

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:28.040
You will be all right.
Don't you think so, Laurette?

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.520
Of course. Your voice it's beautiful.

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:33.070
Thank you, Laurette.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:36.870
Laurette.

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.160
It is a pity, but it's time to go home.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:41.580
I'll have to take Laurette to her home,

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:44.200
but since you live closer,
you can stay a little more.

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:01.540
Right, would you like juice?

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:03.230
I would love it, please.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.730
You seem to like that picture, Anne.

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:56.760
I'm sorry... I...

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:59.650
It's all right, take a good look then.

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:04.960
That is a picture of Jesus blessing the children.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:12.470
When I see this picture,
I remember the time I was alone.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:17.590
I was thinking that I am
like the little girl in the blue dress.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:20.490
No. That I "was" like her.

00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:27.230
Anne Shirley...

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:33.750
By looking at this picture I felt that that girl
hadn't any father or mother of her own.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:37.570
Because she looks lonely and sad.

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:41.900
But she wanted to be blessed, too,

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:45.570
so she just crept shyly up on
the outside of the crowd,

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:50.130
hoping nobody would
notice her except Him.

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:57.050
I'm sure I know just how she felt.

00:19:57.750 --> 00:20:03.460
I felt the same when I asked Miss Marilla
Cuthbert if I could stay in Green Gables.

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:09.250
Her heart must have beat and her
hands must have got cold.

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:14.780
She was afraid He mightn't notice her.

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:18.700
But it's likely He did,
don't you think, Mrs. Allan?

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:21.530
Yes, of course.

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:25.480
I've been trying to imagine it all out.

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:33.330
Her edging a little nearer all the time
until she was quite close to Him,

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:38.220
and then He would look at her and
put His hand on her hair

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:42.340
and such a thrill of joy as
would run over her!

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:50.320
But I wish the artist hadn't painted
Him so sorrowful looking.

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:56.640
I don't believe He could
really have looked so sad

00:20:56.980 --> 00:21:00.530
or the children would have
been afraid of Him.

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:12.070
Anne Shirley, If you could,
could you tell me about your past?

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:13.940
Mrs. Allan...

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:36.430
Marilla!

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:42.220
Oh, Marilla, I've had a most fascinating time.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:45.120
I feel that I have not lived in vain.

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:52.560
I shall always feel like that even if I should
never be invited to tea at a manse again.

00:21:54.790 --> 00:22:00.060
Marilla, I really think I'd like to be a
minister's wife when I grow up.

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:04.970
A minister mightn't mind my red hair

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:08.480
because he wouldn't be
thinking of such worldly things.

00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:14.100
But then of course one would have
to be naturally good,

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:16.430
and I'll never be that.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:19.580
You're heavy.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:28.610
After that, Marilla, Laurette had to go home
and Mrs. Allan and I had a heart-to-heart talk.

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:31.300
I told her everything.

00:22:33.860 --> 00:22:38.090
About Mrs. Thomas and
the twins and Katie Maurice and Violetta.

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:41.890
And coming to Green Gables and
my troubles over geometry.

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:45.730
Mrs. Allan probably
got tired of listening to you.

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:47.820
And would you believe it, Marilla?

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:52.210
Mrs. Allan told me she
was a dunce at geometry too.

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:55.180
Don't you know how much that
encouraged me?

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:57.050
Marilla! Marilla!

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:58.820
You won't believe what I just heard.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:00.430
Big news!

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:02.700
What happened, Rachel?

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:04.560
Don't be to surprised, Marilla.

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:10.710
The trustees have hired
a new teacher and it's a lady.

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:13.880
That's great, my instincts were right.

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:17.100
Then, next September a female teacher will...

00:23:17.270 --> 00:23:19.500
How is she like? Is she young?

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:21.860
This is not a matter of age.

00:23:21.860 --> 00:23:26.550
Avonlea never had
a female teacher before.

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:30.400
I think it is a
dangerous innovation, Marilla.

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:34.730
I think it will be splendid
to have a lady teacher.

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:37.170
Do you know her name, Mrs. Lynde?

00:23:37.240 --> 00:23:41.710
I think... her name is Miss Muriel Stacy.

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:44.420
Miss Muriel Stacy.

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:47.760
What a romantic name.

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:52.570
She must be a gentle and
pretty teacher like Mrs. Allan.

00:23:52.570 --> 00:23:54.960
I hope that the teacher arrives soon.

00:23:56.090 --> 00:23:57.920
I hope September comes fast.

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:01.300
Mrs. Lynde, thank you for the good news.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:05.610
You forgot the bucket.

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:12.610
Anne was really happy.

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:23.610
Anne determines to walk across
the roof after Josie Pye dares her.

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:26.490
Next of Anne of Green Gables:

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:28.400
An Affair of Honor

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:29.490
Please watch!
